          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Burning when I pass urine, plus a temperature and back pain since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what does guidance describe for treatment?",
          "answer": "You should discuss your symptoms with a clinician [diagnostic]. If symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, seek medical advice [diagnostic].\n\nSymptoms like burning or pain on urination, along with a fever and back or flank pain, can be signs of an upper urinary tract infection [condition-urinary-tract-infection]. The presence of fever and flank pain are features that would lead a clinician to consider it more serious than a simple lower urinary tract infection [diagnostic]. These symptoms could mean you have a kidney infection, which is a serious condition [guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment].\n\nRegarding treatment, medical guidance for urinary tract infections (UTIs) differentiates between uncomplicated infections and those that are more serious [guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment]. An \"uncomplicated infection\" is defined as acute cystitis in an adult who is not pregnant, has a normal urinary tract, and normal immune function [guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment]. If you have symptoms such as back pain or a fever, which might suggest kidney involvement, your infection would be considered \"complicated\" [guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment]. Complicated infections, including those with suspected kidney involvement, are managed on a \"different pathway with different antibiotic choices and durations\" compared to uncomplicated infections [guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment]. The specific antibiotic and how long you would need to take it would be determined by a healthcare professional based on your individual situation [guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
